hi there steve here again um remember if  you enjoy these um  videos please subscribe to my channel  today i'm going to talk about a few  things first of all  i had a a skype chat  with aj hoag of effortless english whom  many of you know and i think he's a  great  um enthusiastic teacher  with some wonderful  teaching resources and a great attitude  towards language learning helping people  learn english we had our  skype interview but the quality of the  sound the connection was so poor i'm not  going to put it up  i'll tell you what we talked about we  talked about the problem of people who  plateau in a language and feel they  can't get to the next level so i'm going  to kind of talk a little bit today about  the kinds of things that we discussed  in our video  however  after our video  aj and i then continued our discussion  in a podcast which you can find under  effortless english podcast  and the sound quality was much better  and  it made me realize that you know i would  like to do podcasts because i would like  to talk to people  i would like to talk to people about  language learning not just language  learners not like not just teachers like  aj  but people in different walks of life  with different perspectives why are they  interested in languages how do they go  about learning languages things that  relate to my interest in languages and  we can do them in english and french and  chinese any language that i'm  comfortable conducting the discussion in  so if you are interested or know of  anyone who is interested please let me  know but these will be podcasts they  will not be video because the quality of  the  screen and the the audio is simply not  good enough  and so that's one thing that i will be  starting and i will provide more  information on that later  another small housekeeping item before i  get into the subject of plateauing uh  our many stories project is proceeding  we have  lots of volunteers helping us translate  these many stories into different  languages  uh we need more we need more in a  variety of languages i'd like to see  there  you can follow it at the  forum at link  but in particular we don't have people  for  volunteers for  or enough volunteers or any volunteers  for chinese japanese and korean so today  after this video i'm going to do some  videos in those languages to try to  encourage speakers of those languages to  participate in our project which i'm  very excited about now  the plateau  aj  raised this as something that comes up  all the time people feel that once they  have achieved a certain level and say  english because he basically just  teaches english they have trouble  getting to the next level and i've  discussed this before you know  breakthrough getting to the next level  and we talked about the things that were  important aj pointed out that there is  also a psychological  dimension to this and i agree  basically what i feel is this  there are different kinds of people who  are at a plateau  you can have someone who is at a very  high plateau you can have someone who's  at a low plateau  you can have people who feel that their  say level of english or whatever  language is simply good enough so  they're not bothered by the fact that  their language has reached a level and  it's not going to get much better they  pick up the odd word but by and large  they will continue making the same  grammatical mistakes they will continue  with the same pronunciation and it  doesn't bother them and they're happy  and they go about their lives very often  immigrants fall into this  category  however there are other people who are  unhappy about their level they would  like to speak better they would like to  have you know better pronunciation or  they often find themselves in situations  where they don't understand or they're  unable to express themselves and so  forth so what can these people do well  i think there are a number of things  first of all  i think a lot of people are unduly hard  on themselves it is unrealistic to  expect that you are going to  speak like a native speak with native  pronunciation  never make grammatical mistakes  never find yourself in a situation where  you don't understand that i think is  going to be there so so i you know  i always feel that wherever i am in  language i'm happy that i'm able to do  whatever i'm able to do i never get  unhappy  with what i'm able to do in a language  even those that i  i'm not very good at like romanian or  something  um i'm happy i enjoy what i've been able  to do in the language  so i try to be positive however if i  want to improve  then  i go back to the three keys as always  the attitude of the learner the time you  spend and the ability the ability to  notice your awareness of what's  happening in the language so  to me it's very simple if you want to  improve in the language you have to  begin by wanting to improve you have to  deliberately want to improve  attitude wise you actually have to want  to leave the comfort of your own  cultural environment  as an individual you can't take your  whole  cultural group with you you actually  have to leave that warm fuzzy feeling of  your  might be english-speaking environment or  it might be chinese-speaking environment  or french-speaking environment and you  have to throw yourself in to this other  environment you have to want to do that  you have to want to you want to enjoy  that so that's the psychological element  you have to put in the time if you are a  chinese immigrant living in vancouver  and every evening you watch chinese  television watch chinese videos  that's not going to do it you have to  put in the time you have to read novels  you have to push yourself  watch you know english television so  those kind of things and  you have to improve your ability to  notice notice where your own  pronunciation is you know could be  better could be clearer okay  you don't have to be like a native but  if there are parts of your pronunciation  that are not unclear you have to be  aware of it so you have to listen to  yourself and compare yourself to a  native insofar as pronunciation is  concerned focusing on intonation as much  as anything else  i often refer to my father who said you  know  bank of nova scotia province in canada  it was always nova scotia and work was  vork he came from czechoslovakia it  never bothered him but if he had wanted  to improve he would have had to notice  that actually it's not pronounced scotia  it's pronounced scotia you have to  notice these things and insofar as  grammar is concerned i find it useful  from time to time to review the grammar  whether it be you know i think of  romanian i downloaded the grammar of  romania i have a greek grammar that i  downloaded i go through it i don't try  to memorize but all of these things help  me become aware of the language  better when i listen and read and of  course the biggest thing is in order to  improve get past the plateau you just  have to put in the time lots of  deliberate time reading reading things  that are a little bit challenging on a  wide variety of subjects listening  watching movies  um and then speaking of course and you  have to speak without being hung up but  you have to speak and try to put into  practice try to you know activate the  vocabulary that you have acquired so if  you have this deliberate  policy of of wanting to improve of  pursuing that uh you know effort that  improvement you will improve  however if you're happy with where you  are if you can communicate you work  you uh you know you read you do things  and you have an accent and you make some  mistakes  you're fine i know so many people that i  communicate with in english  um you know and and i've often said this  you know the german person who says i am  living in vancouver since many years  which of course is not correct or the  swedish person says it is many people in  china instead of there are and all of  these things that we bring from our own  language and i'm sure that i do the same  in some of the languages that i speak  but i'm happy so  you know it's all a matter of what you  want to do if you feel you're in on a  plateau and you want to do better you're  going to have to put that effort into it  and you're going to have to be prepared  to leave the comfort of your  native language cultural environment and  you're gonna focus on  as much exposure to the language as  possible both in the form of input and  output with the occasional effort of  looking at some grammar aspects and  seeing if that helps you notice things  uh when you are  either uh you know reading and listening  or using the language so there you have  it with regard to the plateau  any ideas you have about people i could  speak to for my new podcast series we're  going to talk about you know it's a  small world we live in the global  village language  how do people use language why are they  interested in language different  perspectives on the advantages of  learning languages whatever it might be  that's what i want to talk to people  about please let me know  and  that's it  thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]  you
 

